WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:08.975
Okay, we're going to get started
with today's NCBI minute.

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:10.210
Thank you for coming today.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.313
Let me just put in a plug
for our YouTube channel.

00:00:13.313 --> 00:00:18.551
If you subscribe to our channel,
you can get updates of all the

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:20.620
videos we put up on YouTube,
including other tutorials.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:23.723
In terms of the format of this
webinar, our speaker today, Kim

00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:28.395
Pruitt, is going to be showing
you some slides about

00:00:28.395 --> 00:00:30.663
updated prokaryote genome annotation.

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:33.233
If you have questions during
that, go ahead and put those

00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:34.968
into the question pod.

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:38.138
Some of those could be
answered while Kim is talking.

00:00:38.138 --> 00:00:42.308
Others we may answer when she
is finished with the slides.

00:00:42.308 --> 00:00:43.676
Kim will answer those.

00:00:43.676 --> 00:00:47.547
And others yet, we will put in
a document on the "Courses and

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:51.684
Webinars" page at NCBI under
"Materials" where you can see a

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:54.821
log, more or less, of the
questions and answers.

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:58.758
These slides today are already
up on this materials link at the

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:01.661
bottom of the slide
you're seeing right now.

00:01:01.661 --> 00:01:04.230
That goes to the NCBI FTP site.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.267
So without further ado, I'll
introduce Kim Pruitt to talk

00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:11.037
about updates to prokaryotic
genome annotation.

00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:12.305
Okay, thank you everybody.

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:14.974
I'm Kim Pruitt, and I'm the
person who is in charge of

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:16.976
RefSeq karyotes and prokaryotes.

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:21.314
And if you're not familiar with
RefSeq, in a nut shell, it is an

00:01:21.314 --> 00:01:24.617
NCBI project to provide
reference sequence standards

00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:26.953
that incorporate current
knowledge for genomes,

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:28.421
transcripts, and proteins.

00:01:28.421 --> 00:01:31.991
It's a very large dataset, but
today I'm going to talk to you

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:34.961
about some of the new features
in our prokaryotic genomes.

00:01:34.961 --> 00:01:39.365
So just to get started, I want
to give you a sense of how we

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:41.234
create our genomic records.

00:01:41.234 --> 00:01:44.304
These are made basically from
the submitted data that's coming

00:01:44.304 --> 00:01:48.942
into GenBank or our partner
databases in the INSDC, which is

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:52.412
the International Nucleotide Sequence
Database Consortium, or

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:53.480
Collaboration.

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.851
So you as the submitter
community do a lot of work to

00:01:57.851 --> 00:02:01.821
generate genome sequence data
and to assemble that

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:05.758
into the genome sequence
that is submitted to NCBI.

00:02:05.758 --> 00:02:08.628
In some cases you're annotating
that data yourselves,

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:12.432
or you're taking advantage of
NCBI's genome annotation service

00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:14.100
for prokaryotic genomes.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.237
All of that information gets
submitted to GenBank, and it's

00:02:17.237 --> 00:02:20.273
available in our nucleotide
and protein resources.

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:23.076
Sometimes the raw data
is available in SRA.

00:02:23.076 --> 00:02:26.613
The information about the
assembly itself is available in

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:30.383
NCBI's assembly resource, and
metadata about the sample that

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:34.053
was used and about the specific
research project are available

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:36.823
also in our metadata databases.

00:02:36.823 --> 00:02:40.260
The sequence data and the
assembly information is then

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:45.632
copied into the RefSeq project,
where we are then carrying out a

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:47.534
whole genome annotation process.

00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:50.203
This is something that
has changed recently.

00:02:50.203 --> 00:02:55.441
In the prokaryotic world we used
to propagate annotation from the

00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:58.111
submitted GenBank entry, and
only annotate those that did not

00:02:58.111 --> 00:03:04.984
have any annotations, but now we
are annotating all prokaryotic

00:03:04.984 --> 00:03:06.219
genomes using our
annotation pipeline.

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:07.854
This offers us a lot of
improvement in terms of

00:03:07.854 --> 00:03:11.658
consistency of the annotation,
and in terms of being able to

00:03:11.658 --> 00:03:14.627
update them in a more
timely fashion, because some

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:18.331
submissions come in and they're
never updated further to reflect

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:21.668
new information, and so in our
pipeline we can do those kind of

00:03:21.668 --> 00:03:23.036
updates.

00:03:23.036 --> 00:03:25.772
So I'm not going to go into the
details about our pipeline on

00:03:25.772 --> 00:03:29.175
the previous slide that I'm
sorry I moved away from too

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:30.043
quickly.

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:33.313
There was a link to find more
documentation about the pipeline

00:03:33.313 --> 00:03:34.981
process flow itself.

00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:39.218
So our management of the
proteins that we annotate on

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:42.755
prokaryotic records has changed
significantly in the past couple

00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:44.090
of years.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:48.595
The original model that we were
using was that for any given

00:03:48.595 --> 00:03:53.800
assembled genome, and so the
accession prefix is on the top

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:58.404
are two of the accession
prefixes that we use for genomic

00:03:58.404 --> 00:04:00.306
sequence records.

00:04:00.306 --> 00:04:05.812
For any given genome, we used
to provide a distinct accession

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:10.516
number, which I'm showing here
with a YP accession prefix, for

00:04:10.516 --> 00:04:14.821
each annotated coding region
on that genomic sequence.

00:04:14.821 --> 00:04:18.424
So the little pinkish boxes
are coding regions, and the

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:22.495
individual accessions would
be applied per genome.

00:04:22.495 --> 00:04:24.831
So if you had two isolates of

00:04:24.831 --> 00:04:28.968
E. coli from two patients in a
hospital, we would be providing

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:32.372
separate accession numbers for
both of those, so there was no

00:04:32.372 --> 00:04:36.943
way to correlate easily that
the sequences were actually

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:40.346
identical at the protein level,
and we were having a huge

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:44.183
increase in redundancy
in the RefSeq dataset.

00:04:44.183 --> 00:04:47.253
And so we undertook a massive
shift in our data model to

00:04:47.253 --> 00:04:49.522
address the redundancy issue.

00:04:49.522 --> 00:04:53.559
We went through an intermediate
stage where we had our original

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:59.399
accession numbers cross linking
to our new truly non-redundant

00:04:59.399 --> 00:05:02.802
RefSeq protein dataset,
and these currently have an

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:06.272
accession number prefix
that begins with a WP.

00:05:06.272 --> 00:05:10.743
So if you see an accession, WP,
followed by underscore, you know

00:05:10.743 --> 00:05:13.880
you're looking at a truly
non-redundant RefSeq protein

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:15.715
record.

00:05:15.715 --> 00:05:21.287
Our current model, which is the
completion of our transition in

00:05:21.287 --> 00:05:25.758
our protein management, is
that all of our bacterial and

00:05:25.758 --> 00:05:30.496
archival records are directly
annotated with the non-redundant

00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:31.998
protein record.

00:05:31.998 --> 00:05:37.770
So what this means is, for a
given CDS, let's say the first

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:45.311
one on my picture here, if this
protein is identically found in

00:05:45.311 --> 00:05:51.517
five other genomes from the same
or different species, then we

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:56.155
annotate all six of those
genomes with the exact same

00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:59.492
accession number, so there
will be six genomes that are

00:05:59.492 --> 00:06:04.030
providing cross-references to
this identical protein sequence.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:10.036
So we've significantly reduced
the redundancy in our protein datasets.

00:06:10.036 --> 00:06:13.372
You can see some of the actual
measures of that redundancy in

00:06:13.372 --> 00:06:15.174
our documentation page.

00:06:15.174 --> 00:06:17.643
The link is shown at the bottom
of this page, where you just

00:06:17.643 --> 00:06:21.180
plug in the NCBI root URL here.

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:24.117
So this offers us some
advantage in terms of redundancy

00:06:24.117 --> 00:06:29.188
management, but it also has
had a very positive secondary

00:06:29.188 --> 00:06:31.557
benefit in giving us a really
good way to manage protein

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:33.960
names.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.562
We can now manage protein
names directly on this level of

00:06:36.562 --> 00:06:40.099
non-redundant protein records
without having to re-annotate

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:41.601
the genome.

00:06:41.601 --> 00:06:45.638
We can update the protein record
to offer an improved protein

00:06:45.638 --> 00:06:50.042
name, and that improved protein
name is automatically propagated

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:56.149
to the Genome set that protein is annotated on, and this is a

00:06:56.149 --> 00:06:58.785
really strong advantage
to this data model.

00:06:58.785 --> 00:07:03.656
Along the way, we've also added
some categorizations to RefSeq

00:07:03.656 --> 00:07:07.693
prokaryotes, so we now
categorize them as reference

00:07:07.693 --> 00:07:08.895
genomes.

00:07:08.895 --> 00:07:10.396
We have a small number
of reference genomes.

00:07:10.396 --> 00:07:14.066
These are the highest quality
complete genomes that are

00:07:14.066 --> 00:07:20.106
supported by curation, either at
NCBI by NCBI staff scientists,

00:07:20.106 --> 00:07:22.208
or through communities
that we collaborate with.

00:07:22.208 --> 00:07:25.611
So there's greater confidence
in the sequence of these genomes

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:28.948
and in the annotation on these
genomes, and I'm just giving you

00:07:28.948 --> 00:07:29.949
an example.

00:07:29.949 --> 00:07:33.553
Here in my examples, in all
three cases here, the first

00:07:33.553 --> 00:07:37.790
identifier is to NCBI's assembly
database where you can get

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:41.894
metadata about the
quality of the assembly.

00:07:41.894 --> 00:07:46.065
The second identifier is the
RefSeq genome accession number,

00:07:46.065 --> 00:07:48.467
and then followed
by the species name.

00:07:48.467 --> 00:07:52.438
We also have a category called
"representative genomes," and

00:07:52.438 --> 00:07:56.108
what we're trying to do is
select a representative per

00:07:56.108 --> 00:07:57.176
species.

00:07:57.176 --> 00:08:00.046
Most of these representatives
are high quality complete

00:08:00.046 --> 00:08:03.015
genomes, but some of these are
draft, and what we're trying to

00:08:03.015 --> 00:08:07.553
do is select the best genome
available at this point in time.

00:08:07.553 --> 00:08:10.156
These are subject to change
over time as we get more genome

00:08:10.156 --> 00:08:11.757
sequencing data.

00:08:11.757 --> 00:08:14.861
And for lack of a better word,
we're currently calling the

00:08:14.861 --> 00:08:18.898
remainder our "variant genome."
The majority of these represent

00:08:18.898 --> 00:08:22.401
bacterial species for which
there have been thousands and

00:08:22.401 --> 00:08:26.172
thousands of sequences
submitted for the same organism.

00:08:26.172 --> 00:08:27.740
So we may have thousands of

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:31.510
E. coli genome submissions,
thousands of salmonella genome

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:34.914
submissions, and these are
really defining variants of the

00:08:34.914 --> 00:08:37.416
species.

00:08:37.416 --> 00:08:41.254
You can find information about
these categories referenced or

00:08:41.254 --> 00:08:44.824
representative by searching
our assembly resource, and here

00:08:44.824 --> 00:08:50.529
is an example query to find
RefSeq reference genomes.

00:08:50.529 --> 00:08:54.033
So this is a picture, a screen
shot of one of our non-redundant

00:08:54.033 --> 00:08:57.670
protein records, and you can see
it has the WP accession number

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:00.006
showing up here on the record.

00:09:00.006 --> 00:09:04.176
I want to point out that these
records are annotated at the

00:09:04.176 --> 00:09:07.713
lowest common denominator
in terms of what organism

00:09:07.713 --> 00:09:09.649
information they carry.

00:09:09.649 --> 00:09:14.854
So if there is a -- in this
case, this is an extreme case,

00:09:14.854 --> 00:09:19.125
this is something that is
annotated on genomes of

00:09:19.125 --> 00:09:22.895
different species, where the
lowest common denominator of

00:09:22.895 --> 00:09:26.465
those species is bacteria,
and so that is the organism

00:09:26.465 --> 00:09:29.902
information that is carried
on this sequence record.

00:09:29.902 --> 00:09:32.738
You might find others that are
carrying organism information at

00:09:32.738 --> 00:09:35.975
the level of the genus, and, of
course, there are still many that

00:09:35.975 --> 00:09:38.611
carry organism information
at the level of the species.

00:09:38.611 --> 00:09:42.515
There's more information about
the non-redundant protein

00:09:42.515 --> 00:09:44.216
sequences available online, and
at the top of the page is the

00:09:44.216 --> 00:09:46.686
URL to that documentation.

00:09:46.686 --> 00:09:49.588
At the bottom of the page is
the URL to the specific protein

00:09:49.588 --> 00:09:51.157
record that I'm
showing you here.

00:09:51.157 --> 00:09:56.529
Very recently we've added a
database that is being used to

00:09:56.529 --> 00:10:00.032
manage protein names, and so
within that database we have

00:10:00.032 --> 00:10:03.469
stored all of the protein names
that are currently used for our

00:10:03.469 --> 00:10:05.972
non-redundant protein records.

00:10:05.972 --> 00:10:08.708
We have stored all of
the Swiss protein names.

00:10:08.708 --> 00:10:14.013
We are collecting and generating
a large dataset of HMMs.

00:10:14.013 --> 00:10:16.515
We are doing protein clustering.

00:10:16.515 --> 00:10:19.852
We are looking at identical
proteins, and we are curating

00:10:19.852 --> 00:10:20.686
names.

00:10:20.686 --> 00:10:24.623
And so we are embarked, in the
past couple of months, on a very

00:10:24.623 --> 00:10:28.794
massive initiative to improve
the protein names on these

00:10:28.794 --> 00:10:32.832
records and to standardize the
way we're formatting protein

00:10:32.832 --> 00:10:36.736
names and these records, and so
you should look for improvements

00:10:36.736 --> 00:10:38.671
to continue to come through
the course of this year.

00:10:38.671 --> 00:10:41.440
And I can't spend too
much more time on this.

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:44.944
I think I'm over my webinar ten
minutes, but if you'll bear with

00:10:44.944 --> 00:10:47.546
me for a couple more minutes,
this is a picture of a new

00:10:47.546 --> 00:10:51.017
webpage display called the
"Identical Protein Report."

00:10:51.017 --> 00:10:52.985
You can get to this from
any protein record.

00:10:52.985 --> 00:10:56.856
There's a link at the top of the
page in the same location as the

00:10:56.856 --> 00:11:01.494
GenPept FASTA graphics link,
shown here, to access this page.

00:11:01.494 --> 00:11:03.496
This is a very useful page.

00:11:03.496 --> 00:11:07.299
It tells you how many genomes
a given protein record was

00:11:07.299 --> 00:11:08.367
annotated on.

00:11:08.367 --> 00:11:10.169
In this case I've boxed three.

00:11:10.169 --> 00:11:14.673
So this same protein accession
number has been annotated on

00:11:14.673 --> 00:11:18.644
three different RefSeq genomes,
the accessions are shown here,

00:11:18.644 --> 00:11:21.580
and the coordinates of the
annotation are shown here.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:25.117
The organism information is
shown in the column to the

00:11:25.117 --> 00:11:28.754
right, and so you can see that
these are three very different

00:11:28.754 --> 00:11:31.490
bacterial organisms, and the
lowest common denominator for

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:35.061
these is at the bacteria level.

00:11:35.061 --> 00:11:38.431
In some cases, this may be
an indicator that there was a

00:11:38.431 --> 00:11:42.435
problem in identifying
the correct organism for a

00:11:42.435 --> 00:11:45.604
submission, but in other cases
this is really reflecting very

00:11:45.604 --> 00:11:47.940
interesting biology.

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:51.510
And last but not least, in the
process of converting to our new

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:54.346
data model, there was an
awful lot of suppression that

00:11:54.346 --> 00:11:55.347
happened.

00:11:55.347 --> 00:11:59.752
We suppressed the intermediate
level of records, the YP and NP

00:11:59.752 --> 00:12:02.354
accession numbers on all of
our representative and variant

00:12:02.354 --> 00:12:04.023
genomes.

00:12:04.023 --> 00:12:06.892
I just want to make a note that
for the reference genomes they

00:12:06.892 --> 00:12:10.529
continue to use the data model
where there is an independent

00:12:10.529 --> 00:12:15.434
accession defined for those,
for the reference genome.

00:12:15.434 --> 00:12:18.270
For those records that were
suppressed, we have provided a

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:20.306
custom message on those records.

00:12:20.306 --> 00:12:25.511
So if you go to this URL, you
will see a very informative

00:12:25.511 --> 00:12:29.348
message provided that tells you
that this accession that has

00:12:29.348 --> 00:12:33.052
been suppressed has, indeed,
been replaced by a non-redundant

00:12:33.052 --> 00:12:34.453
protein record.

00:12:34.453 --> 00:12:37.923
And you can navigate to that
protein record to the identical

00:12:37.923 --> 00:12:39.959
protein report or the
documentation about our "Me

00:12:39.959 --> 00:12:45.197
Imitation Project." And last,
there is a comprehensive mapping

00:12:45.197 --> 00:12:47.967
file provided for the those
who would like to have a

00:12:47.967 --> 00:12:49.802
comprehensive report by FTP.

00:12:49.802 --> 00:12:54.306
This file is available in
the RefSeq Release Catalog

00:12:54.306 --> 00:12:55.207
Directory.

00:12:55.207 --> 00:12:57.776
This was put out in the
beginning of this month.

00:12:57.776 --> 00:13:01.914
In July, when we process our
next RefSeq release, this file

00:13:01.914 --> 00:13:05.050
will be moved into the Release
Catalog Archive Directory where

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:07.686
it will continue
to be available.

00:13:07.686 --> 00:13:10.789
This file provides mapping
between the old suppressed

00:13:10.789 --> 00:13:14.960
protein accession number to
the alive non-redundant protein

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:17.596
accession that is replacing it.

00:13:17.596 --> 00:13:20.599
It also maps old locus
tags to new locus tags.

00:13:20.599 --> 00:13:23.836
We had to annotate our genomes
with new locus tags because we

00:13:23.836 --> 00:13:27.273
are now providing de novo
genome annotation for all of our

00:13:27.273 --> 00:13:30.309
records, and there are some new
genes that are being called.

00:13:30.309 --> 00:13:33.312
So we cannot rely on existing
locus tags that may have been

00:13:33.312 --> 00:13:34.647
submitted.

00:13:34.647 --> 00:13:38.484
It also reports information on
GeneID mapping for people who

00:13:38.484 --> 00:13:41.287
are interested in that
information from NCBI's gene

00:13:41.287 --> 00:13:42.655
resource.

00:13:42.655 --> 00:13:47.726
And I would just like to thank
people who have done all of the

00:13:47.726 --> 00:13:48.761
work on these changes.

00:13:48.761 --> 00:13:51.063
If you have questions, you can
look at the online documentation

00:13:51.063 --> 00:13:54.533
that I provided links
to in these slides.

00:13:54.533 --> 00:13:58.370
You can also look at our FAQ
or you can contact us at either

00:13:58.370 --> 00:14:03.108
info@ncbi or webinars@ncbi
to follow up with questions.

00:14:03.108 --> 00:14:04.610
Thank you.

00:14:07.446 --> 00:14:10.516
So the question is what is
the source of the annotations

00:14:10.516 --> 00:14:13.052
qualifiers attached to the
annotation of non-redundant

00:14:13.052 --> 00:14:14.820
protein entry, and what is the
procedure if I have a question

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:17.089
about that annotation?

00:14:17.089 --> 00:14:19.692
So are you talking about -- I'm
not sure exactly what you mean

00:14:19.692 --> 00:14:20.759
by qualifiers.

00:14:20.759 --> 00:14:22.595
Are you talking about the
organism information, the

00:14:22.595 --> 00:14:25.564
protein name, or something else?

00:14:25.564 --> 00:14:28.367
Oh, the product, so you're
talking about the protein name.

00:14:28.367 --> 00:14:32.137
So, initially the source was
what came in from GenBank.

00:14:32.137 --> 00:14:36.809
NCBI have been working on
correcting clustering and naming

00:14:36.809 --> 00:14:40.346
things that are similar by
homology for a couple of years.

00:14:40.346 --> 00:14:44.617
In the beginning of March we
created this new name management

00:14:44.617 --> 00:14:49.221
database that really gives us
a lot of power to improve our

00:14:49.221 --> 00:14:52.524
protein names, and we have
already improved about 1.2

00:14:52.524 --> 00:14:56.595
million protein names, and this
is an area that we are extremely

00:14:56.595 --> 00:14:58.364
actively working on.

00:14:58.364 --> 00:15:02.601
So if you have specific name
examples, then, yes, please send

00:15:02.601 --> 00:15:06.772
them, or specific names that are
bad, then, yes, please send that

00:15:06.772 --> 00:15:07.973
information to us.

00:15:07.973 --> 00:15:11.510
We're very, very interested in
improving the quality of the

00:15:11.510 --> 00:15:14.647
names on our non-redundant
protein records, and you can use

00:15:14.647 --> 00:15:20.586
the info@ncbi e-mail
to contact us for that.

00:15:20.586 --> 00:15:22.421
Okay, that looks like
all of the questions.

00:15:22.421 --> 00:15:25.557
If you think of something else,
go ahead and send those to the

00:15:25.557 --> 00:15:29.828
webinars@ncbi e-mail address,
and we'll get those included in

00:15:29.828 --> 00:15:31.930
the documentation
for this webinar.

00:15:31.930 --> 00:15:32.965
Thank you very much.

00:15:32.965 --> 00:15:35.301
We're going to sign off now, and
we'll see you at the next NCBI

00:15:35.301 --> 00:15:36.802
minute.

00:15:36.802 --> 00:15:37.736
Goodbye.

